Estimates based on experimentally determined ligandomes exceed one million peptides required to train a generalizable model of TCR specificity
To provide lower bound estimates for the amount of training data required for a generalizable TCRpMHC specificity model, expanded experimental ligandome dominating set sizes at edit distances 1, 2 and 3 were calculated for 34 alleles (see Section “
Experimental ligandome estimations
”). These 34 alleles were chosen to maximize population coverage (see Section “
MHC-I Allele Selection
”).
Large inter-allele variation was seen for dominating sets at all edit distance thresholds (Fig.
3
). Dominating sets at edit distance 1 showed the largest variation; A*02:01’s dominating set was estimated at over 100,000 unique 9-mers, whilst B*52:01’s dominating set estimation was fewer than 3,000 unique peptides. Dominating sets solved at edit distance 1 were an order of magnitude larger than those solved at edit distances 2 and 3.
Fig. 3
Expanded experimental ligandome dominating set sizes across edit distance thresholds 1–3 for 34 representative MHC-I alleles. Allele A*02:01 is highlighted in red.
Expanded experimental ligandome dominating sets for all 34 alleles were combined into a pan-allelic dominating set (Table
1
). These values represent a lower, experimentally supported bound of the minimum number of unique peptides required to train a classifier capable of generalizing across a given edit distance threshold. For example, if a given model architecture can generalize to peptides of edit distance 3 or below from the training set, only 135,000 peptides are required for training. Again, we considered a peptide-allele pair as a unique data point; identical peptides present in dominating sets for multiple alleles were all counted as separate instances due to the importance of TCR-MHC interactions.
