Fivefold cross-validation experiment.
To rule out the possibility that our results on a modified TULIP split were split-specific, we additionally conducted a separate 5-fold cross-validation experiment. Positive examples from the entire TULIP dataset were split randomly into 5 folds with equal counts of unique and non-overlapping peptides. Then, in a loop over the folds, each fold was treated as a testing split and the remaining 4 folds combined - as a training split. The same filtering of the shared TCRs and imputation of the negative pairs was applied to each testing / training pair of splits, followed by training the models on the training splits and evaluation of the combined performance on the testing splits as described below.
Predictive models
In our benchmark, we evaluate five models, prioritizing diverse architectural approaches over exhaustive benchmarking. This selection process allows for a comprehensive exploration of available architectures. In particular, we retained ATM-TCR
12
, TCR-H
14
and TULIP
11
, as well as two standard machine learning baselines (XGBoost and Random Forest).
ATM-TCR.
The ATM-TCR model
12
predicts binding affinity between TCR and epitope sequences using multi-head self-attention mechanisms to capture contextual information within sequences and understanding of amino acid interactions. Each residue is represented as a one-hot vector and processed through embedding layers. The encoded sequences then pass through corresponding encoders with multi-head self-attention, and the model’s decoder computes binding affinity by combining encoded representations through linear layers and activation functions. Our preliminary training revealed that the model’s predicted probabilities exhibited poor calibration, predominantly clustering around 0.99 for unseen peptides. Consequently, following Mukhoti et al.
26
we opted to retrain the model employing focal loss, resulting in significantly improved calibration of outcomes. We note that our improvement in the score calibration has not resulted in any deterioration of the predictive performance metrics in our comparative benchmarking against the original implementation on the same dataset.
Random forest.
A Random Forest is an ensemble machine learning method that combines multiple decision trees to make predictions
27
. It works by constructing a multitude of decision trees during training and then averaging or voting on their outputs during prediction. Each tree in the forest is trained on a random subset of the data, making the model robust and capable of handling complex datasets. In this study, we employ BLOSUM50 embeddings
28
as input features, representing each CDR3
