TULIP.
TULIP
11
is another transformer-based model using three encoders and decoders to focus on key interacting regions within epitopes, MHCs, and TCR chains. Contrary to most TCR specificity models, TULIP is trained in an unsupervised way as a generative model, as it does not explicitly predict a binary binding variable but is trained to maximize the likelihood of observing a given component of a TCR-peptide-MHC tuple from the positive examples (e.g. the peptide) conditioned on observing the other two components (e.g. TCR and MHC allele). This approach circumvents the need to create artificial non-binding pairs for training but not for evaluation.
XGBoost.
XGBoost is a gradient boosting algorithm used for supervised learning tasks such as classification and regression
30
. It builds an ensemble of decision trees sequentially, with each tree focusing on the errors of the previous one. XGBoost optimizes performance by employing techniques like gradient boosting and regularization. XGBoost has become a popular choice in various machine learning competitions and real-world applications due to its significant predictive performance and flexibility. Our XGBoost model employs the same input features as the Random Forest model.
Training and hyper-parameters.
We train ATM-TCR, TCR-H and TULIP using the hyper-parameters and training settings provided in their official implementations
11
,
12
,
14
. For the Random Forest we use 40 estimators without extensive fine-tuning. Similarly, for XGBoost models, we use 100 estimators and a learning rate of 0.1.
Predictive metrics and their confidence intervals
We computed the Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve and its corresponding Area Under the Curve (AUC) value for each model providing an overall assessment of their performance on unseen peptides across folds. To refine our analysis, we stratified the results based on the similarity of peptides to the training set. More precisely, we calculated the edit distance between each test peptide and its nearest counterpart in the training set. We then report the ROC-AUC stratified per minimum dissimilarity (edit distance) between each testing peptide and peptides in the training dataset. We also report the Precision-Recall Area Under the Curve (PR-AUC), which measures the area under the precision-recall curve to evaluate the performance of binary classification models.
To quantify uncertainty while respecting dependence among test examples that share a peptide, we estimated confidence intervals (CIs) using a balanced peptide–cluster bootstrap. In each of 5000 replicates, we (i) sampled each peptide once (without replacement), (ii) resampled its TCR pairs (both positive and negative pairs as one pool) with replacement matching the original count of TCRs for that peptide, and (iii) pooled resampled examples from all peptides and computed ROC–AUC (binned by edit distance and count of training set TCRs as described above). To limit dominance by peptides with many test TCRs, we used capped per–peptide weights in the AUC: for peptide
