In the case of the 5-fold cross-validation experiment, the resampling procedure described above was used to generate 5000 ROC-AUC values per test dataset in each of the 5 folds. Then, 5000 new replicates were generated by randomly sampling with replacement a single ROC-AUC value from each of the folds, resulting in 5000 tuples of 5 values each. These 5 values were averaged using as weight the count of resampled examples per fold which were used to compute each value. Percentile intervals were taken over the final 5000 bootstrapped ROC-AUC values to compute 90% CIs. The motivation of using this nested bootstrap approach is that the alternative of pooling the resampled examples across folds and computing ROC-AUC would have resulted in comparing scores from models trained on different, although overlapping, subsets of data. Fold-specific scores can be on different scales and calibrations. ROC–AUC is invariant to a global monotone transform, but not to piecewise transforms that differ by fold, so cross-fold pooling can distort the cross-peptide ranking and bias the estimate.
