Non-self ligandome estimations
To estimate the lower bound for each allele’s set of potential class-I human tumor neoantigens, we aimed to generate all point mutations of normal proteome peptides which are presented by the MHC alleles dominant in the population. Such SNV-derived mutations comprise the majority of targets which can be identified with currently available approaches to personalized tumor epitope discovery. We then added known viral and tumor origin epitopes presented by these alleles. A generalizable TCR-pMHC specificity prediction model would have to work on peptide targets from this set, which we call a “non-self ligandome”. The non-self ligandome estimations were performed in an allele-wise manner via two distinct methods. Expanded experimental ligandomes for each selected allele were based on existing experimental binding data and the local experimental mutanome (See Section “
Experimental ligandome estimations
”). Predicted ligandomes were calculated based on exhaustive synthetic point mutations introduced into the human proteome and augmented with verified tumor neoantigens with no associated MHC binding data (See Section “
Predicted ligandome estimations
”). For each allele, we further calculated a set of unique peptides from both predicted ligandomes and expanded experimental ligandomes termed the union ligandome (See Section “
Predicted ligandome estimations
”). A full overview of the workflow for the construction of the various ligandomes produced in this study is available in the supplementary information (Supplementary Fig.
9
).
MHC-I allele selection
As ligandome estimates were performed allele-wise, we aimed to select a representative set of MHC-I alleles to ensure sufficient coverage of loci A, B and C in the world population. To obtain this representative set, HLA allele frequencies for 21 ethnic populations were obtained from Gragert et al.
32
. To better facilitate general population coverage estimates, ethnic groups were further subset to six broad populations: Caucasian (‘CAU’), African American (‘AFA’), Hispanic (‘HIS’), Asian & Pacific Islands (‘API’), Northern Chinese (‘NCHI’), and Asian Indian (‘AINDI’). For each included population, the five most common alleles at each locus A, B and C were selected as members of the representative set. Coverage was estimated as a fraction of the individuals within a given ethnic group with at least one of the alleles found in their diploid genotype. Duplicate alleles between categories were removed, resulting in 34 representative MHC-I alleles present in between 56% and 99% of individuals in a given population (Supplementary Fig.
10
). We note that there is a possible alternative definition of coverage requiring all alleles in the MHC-I genotype of an individual to be contained in the chosen set of alleles. That population coverage is much lower from the same set of alleles and not discussed further in this work.
Experimental ligandome estimations
To obtain all available experimentally verified binding peptides for a given MHC allele, we queried multiple publicly available immunopeptidomic databases as of 2023-08-09. All experimental binders from the HLA Ligand Atlas were retrieved from the 2020.12 release
33
. The Immune Epitope Database (IEDB) was queried for all human pMHC binding assays involving healthy, tumor or viral origin peptides
15
. Peptide-MHC binding data was additionally retrieved from the NetMHCPan 4.1 training data
8
. This data consists of pMHC interaction data derived from both binding affinity assays and mass spectrometry experiments. While this dataset in its entirety contains over 13M data points, many entries are from multi-allelic assays and lack annotation with a specific MHC I allele. Hence, we kept only monoallelic data for our analysis. Due to the scarcity of experimental pMHC binding data for viral origin peptides in IEDB, we further supplemented our experimentally verified pMHC assays with viral origin peptides from VDJdb
16
. Lastly, we include all peptides labelled as binders from multiple in-house mass spectrometry assays [Internal proteomics dataset]. From these collected data, all unique 9-mer peptides were extracted; we subsequently refer to this unique subset as the experimental ligandome throughout (Supplementary Table
1
).
To verify that peptides labelled as normal self were indeed of human origin, we performed a BLAST
34
search of all peptides not explicitly labelled as tumor or viral in origin against the UniProt human reference proteome
35
. All peptides labelled as normal self without probable BLAST matches were excluded from all subsequent analysis. For this task, we defined a probable match as a sequence within one edit distance of any 9-mer present in the human reference proteome.
As the subject of this article is the estimation of the TCR-reactive mutanome, we then synthesized a ‘local experimental mutanome’. To produce this local experimental mutanome, we enumerated all possible 9-mer peptides within edit distance 1 of any normal self origin (or tumor origin) peptide in the experimental ligandome. In this way, we created a set of peptides which are both very close to experimentally verified binders for a given allele, and most likely to arise via single-residue mutations to healthy or existing tumor tissues. The latter was treated this way under an assumption that the vast majority of MHC ligands identified or predicted from the tumor tissue are still normal (non-mutated) peptides. For a given allele, the local experimental mutanome was passed through NetMHCPan 4.1 and all peptides predicted as strong binders kept. Lastly, all predicted binders from the local mutanome or peptides from the experimental ligandome with exact 9-mer matches to the human reference proteome were removed. All remaining peptides from the experimental mutanome and predicted binders from the local experimental mutanome were combined into a final ‘expanded experimental ligandome’. This subset contains only non-normal-self peptides and viral peptides, and aims to represent all putative TCR-reactive peptides binding a given MHC allele with some experimental evidence. We treat this expanded experimental ligandome as a lower bound estimate for the number of non-normal-self peptides a given MHC allele may bind.
Predicted ligandome estimations
For each allele in our representative set, we aimed to estimate the upper bound of all non-normal-self point mutation peptides they may bind. To achieve this, we created an ‘exhaustive human 9-mer mutanome’ from the UniProt reference human proteome
35
. All unique 9-mer peptides (n= 10.2M) were identified using a 9-mer sliding window approach across all 20,408 genes in the reference proteome. All unique single residue mutations were enumerated from these 9-mers, excluding mutations which resulted in the creation of another existing normal self peptide. Hence, the exhaustive human 9-mer mutanome contains all non-normal-self 9-mer peptides which may arise from a single residue mutation in any position in the human proteome (n= 1.73B).
Additionally, we obtained all entries from TSNAdb 2.0, a database of experimentally verified human tumor neoantigens from tumor sample sequence data
36
. As this database contained peptides of varying length, we excluded all peptides with length <9. All peptides of length 9 are included unmodified, whilst peptides of length >9 were split into all possible linear 9-mer binding cores using a sliding window approach. For example, an 11-mer peptide would be present as three unique 9-mers consisting of residues 0:9, residues 1:10, and residues 2:11. All TSNAdb 2.0 peptides with exact matches to the UniProt human reference proteome were excluded, ensuring TSNAdb data represented known non-normal-self human tumor neoantigens.
All remaining peptides from both the exhaustive human 9-mer proteome and TSNAdb 2.0 were passed through NetMHCPan 4.1 and strong binders kept. Hence, these binders aim to represent all possible mutant 9-mer peptides a given MHC allele may bind; we refer to these predicted binders throughout as an allele’s ‘predicted ligandome’. For a given MHC allele, these predicted binders were then combined with all peptides from that allele’s expanded experimental ligandome. The unique subset of this combination represents an exhaustive upper bound for the allele’s ligandome, and is subsequently referred to as the ‘union ligandome’ throughout.
Dominating set estimations
For any given set of peptides, we use the ‘dominating set’ as a coverage measure of the peptide space. In graph based approaches, the dominating set is defined as a subset of nodes (or vertices -
V
) such that every node in the graph is either a member of this dominating set, or adjacent (connected by an edge -
E
) to a node which is a member of this dominating set
37
.
