The size of this set represents the training sample size required for generalization. Let us suppose that a TCR-pMHC binding model can generalize to peptides 1 edit distance from any training examples, but not further. Figure
5
shows how a valid dominating set consisting of a single peptide can be used as sufficient training data to train a model that can generalize to predict TCR specificity for any peptide from the entire graph. By constructing these peptide graphs and solving the minimum dominating sets for a given allele’s ligandome, we therefore estimate the minimum number of peptides required to train a model that can generalize across the entire considered ligandome at a given edit distance.
Fig. 5
Example of valid and invalid dominating sets for a peptide graph with edges drawn at edit distance one. The left hand graph is an invalid solution at edit distance one (AAGIALLTV and AAGIAELTV are not covered by AAGIGILTV at edit distance one), but would be valid at edit distance two. The right hand graph shows a valid optimal solution (all peptides are covered by AAGIAILTV at edit distance one); this single peptide (AAGIAILTV) would therefore be sufficient epitope training data for a model to generalize to the entire graph at edit distance one.
To enable graph construction, all pairwise edit distances for considered peptides were determined heuristically using the SACHICA algorithm
23
. Peptide graphs for a given ligandome were constructed using the NetworkX Python package
24
. Edges between peptides were considered at edit distance thresholds 1, 2 and 3. As determining the true minimum dominating set is an NP-complete problem, dominating sets for constructed graphs were obtained heuristically using a modified version of the NetworkX package’s
min_weighted_dominating_set
function. Calculated dominating sets were verified as true dominating sets using the NetworkX
is_dominating_set
function. To reduce the computational complexity of greedily solving the dominating set for a given graph, we first removed isolated nodes before dominating set calculations were undertaken. Here, isolated nodes were peptides where no other peptide in the considered ligandome was within the edit distance threshold; these peptides are manually added to final dominating sets after their calculation.
To determine overall pan-allelic dominating set sizes, we take the sum (not the union) of all union ligandome sizes across all 34 alleles; identical peptides present in the union ligandomes for two different MHC alleles are considered as separate data points due to the importance of TCR-MHC interactions in driving the specific TCR recognition of the epitopes.
Union ligandome subsampling
Despite the use of heuristics, determining valid dominating sets for union ligandomes of millions of peptides was computationally intractable. Therefore, to estimate union ligandome dominating set sizes, we randomly subsampled up to 50% of the union ligandome peptides from A*02:01 and solved the dominating sets at edit distance thresholds 1, 2 and 3 (Fig.
4
a). From this subsampling, we fitted an asymptotic curve to both interpolate and extrapolate the dominating set size estimation as necessary for the other alleles. We assume parameters obtained for A*02:01 broadly apply to all considered alleles and hence estimate dominating set sizes for the remaining 34 alleles using this approach.

Experimental ligandome estimations
To obtain all available experimentally verified binding peptides for a given MHC allele, we queried multiple publicly available immunopeptidomic databases as of 2023-08-09. All experimental binders from the HLA Ligand Atlas were retrieved from the 2020.12 release
33
. The Immune Epitope Database (IEDB) was queried for all human pMHC binding assays involving healthy, tumor or viral origin peptides
15
. Peptide-MHC binding data was additionally retrieved from the NetMHCPan 4.1 training data
8
. This data consists of pMHC interaction data derived from both binding affinity assays and mass spectrometry experiments. While this dataset in its entirety contains over 13M data points, many entries are from multi-allelic assays and lack annotation with a specific MHC I allele. Hence, we kept only monoallelic data for our analysis. Due to the scarcity of experimental pMHC binding data for viral origin peptides in IEDB, we further supplemented our experimentally verified pMHC assays with viral origin peptides from VDJdb
16
. Lastly, we include all peptides labelled as binders from multiple in-house mass spectrometry assays [Internal proteomics dataset]. From these collected data, all unique 9-mer peptides were extracted; we subsequently refer to this unique subset as the experimental ligandome throughout (Supplementary Table
1
).
To verify that peptides labelled as normal self were indeed of human origin, we performed a BLAST
34
search of all peptides not explicitly labelled as tumor or viral in origin against the UniProt human reference proteome
35
. All peptides labelled as normal self without probable BLAST matches were excluded from all subsequent analysis. For this task, we defined a probable match as a sequence within one edit distance of any 9-mer present in the human reference proteome.
As the subject of this article is the estimation of the TCR-reactive mutanome, we then synthesized a ‘local experimental mutanome’. To produce this local experimental mutanome, we enumerated all possible 9-mer peptides within edit distance 1 of any normal self origin (or tumor origin) peptide in the experimental ligandome. In this way, we created a set of peptides which are both very close to experimentally verified binders for a given allele, and most likely to arise via single-residue mutations to healthy or existing tumor tissues. The latter was treated this way under an assumption that the vast majority of MHC ligands identified or predicted from the tumor tissue are still normal (non-mutated) peptides. For a given allele, the local experimental mutanome was passed through NetMHCPan 4.1 and all peptides predicted as strong binders kept. Lastly, all predicted binders from the local mutanome or peptides from the experimental ligandome with exact 9-mer matches to the human reference proteome were removed. All remaining peptides from the experimental mutanome and predicted binders from the local experimental mutanome were combined into a final ‘expanded experimental ligandome’. This subset contains only non-normal-self peptides and viral peptides, and aims to represent all putative TCR-reactive peptides binding a given MHC allele with some experimental evidence. We treat this expanded experimental ligandome as a lower bound estimate for the number of non-normal-self peptides a given MHC allele may bind.

