Introduction
The adaptive immune system is a critical part of the immune response to infections and tumor cells. It contains a diverse set of T lymphocytes, each having surface receptors targeting specific antigens. This study focuses on antigens presented to cytotoxic T cells, specifically tumor neoantigens; novel mutant peptides derived from normal self tissues via somatic mutations during tumorigenesis and tumor progression. For antigen presentation, a mutated protein within the cell undergoes cleavage by the proteasome into peptides. Subsequently, these peptides translocate to the endoplasmic reticulum, where they can bind to the class-I Major Histocompatibility Complex (MHC) to form a peptide-MHC protein complex (pMHC)
1
(Fig.
1
a–c). The resulting pMHC complex may eventually migrate to the cell surface, where it becomes accessible for recognition by a cytotoxic T cell receptor (TCR). The binding of the TCR to the pMHC is a necessary but not sufficient condition for T cell activation, which is also dependent on other biological factors such as mediator proteins
1
. Class-I MHC proteins are composed of two chains, alpha and beta. The alpha chain forms a binding groove resembling a pocket to which the peptide is anchored, leaving middle residues exposed for TCR recognition
2
. In contrast, the beta(2)-microglobulin chain is not in direct interaction with the peptide. The peptide sequence is typically 8-12 amino acids long for class-I MHCs. The TCR consists of two chains with highly variable regions known as Complementarity-Determining Region (CDR) loops. In particular, the third CDR of the
