Accurate prediction of TCR-pMHC specificity would be highly beneficial for designing personalized T cell receptor-based cancer immunotherapies
5
,
6
without requiring a lengthy experimental screening process. Such personalized therapy can cover a very broad patient population. Unlike models which rely on the observed phenotype of T cells in the patient to identify likely tumor-reactive TCR clones
5
, prediction of specificity based on TCR sequence would not be affected by confounding factors such as prior treatment history and differences between cancer indications. Each patient has a largely unique set of TCRs generated through the process of random recombination during thymic development. Moreover, the neoantigens exposed on tumor cells are mostly unique to each individual
7
, with each neoantigen presented by MHC molecules which also differ between patients. Thus, a useful model that predicts TCR-pMHC binding specificity for personalized TCR-based treatment should generalize to previously unseen peptide-MHC complexes and TCRs. Machine learning (ML) models learn patterns from the training data of known examples in order to make predictions for new data. Deep learning (DL) models have had notable success in recent years to predict protein interactions by learning statistical patterns from large datasets of biological sequences. NetMHCPan 4.1
8
and MHCFlurry
9
are robust predictors of peptide-MHC binding. Recently, several models including ERGO
10
, TULIP
11
, ATM-TCR
12
, PiTE
13
and TCR-H
14
have tried to leverage datasets of reactive TCR-pMHC sequences as training data to predict TCR-pMHC binding specificity using DL models. However, although efforts have been made to collect more TCR-pMHC binding data, the most widely used sequence-based databases such as IEDB
15
, VDJdb
16
and McPasTCR
17
exhibit a low number of unique peptides compared to the number of TCRs, with a very uneven distribution of TCRs across peptides, and they lack the experimental examples of nonbinding pairs. This could introduce a bias into both training and evaluation procedures and lead to an overestimation of generalization capabilities on unseen data.
In this study, we benchmarked several representative TCR-pMHC binding specificity models for their predictive accuracy on novel peptide epitopes and TCRs using a carefully constructed dataset to eliminate possible causes of inflated performance. Our findings revealed a universal shortcoming in the ability of these models to accurately predict TCR-binding specificity for previously unseen peptides, which was also found for earlier models in the previous benchmarking efforts
11
,
18
. From this stage of the analysis, we derived the maximum sequence dissimilarity between evaluation and training peptides at which the performance remains acceptable. Based on this result, we aimed to determine how many training data samples would be needed to cover the entire space of potential human tumor neoantigens primarily derived from single nucleotide variants (SNVs) in the coding sequence and presented on MHC class-I molecules. We focus the estimation on SNVs for computational tractability and because SNVs in the protein-coding regions comprise the majority of candidate targets identified by the current methods which discover personalized tumor neoantigens. To achieve this, we formulated the problem as a graph where each vertex represents a peptide sequence in the space of tumor neoantigens and each edge connects peptides within the specified edit distance from each other. Leveraging graph theory, we identified a dominating set, defined as a subset of vertices, ensuring that every vertex in the graph is either within the set or adjacent to it. The calculated dominating set represents the minimum set of peptides required for training a sequence-based ML model with acceptable performance on the unseen epitopes. The overall design of our sample size estimation is illustrated on (Supplementary Fig.
1
)
To the best of our knowledge, this is the first attempt at estimating the sufficient training data size for this important and intensely studied prediction problem. This estimation represents the main novel contribution of our work.
Results
Specificity prediction models fail to generalize to unseen epitopes
Here, we assessed the performance of several published models which aim to predict whether a given TCR-pMHC pair will bind. We began by comparing their average performance on peptides unseen during training, using the dataset split described in Section “
TCR-pMHC binding data
”). Figure
2
a and b showcase the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (ROC-AUC) and area under the precision recall curve (PR-AUC) metrics on unseen peptides. All models demonstrated poor generalization capabilities when presented with unseen peptides, performing close to a random classifier with a ROC-AUC of 0.5. The uniformity of performance variance among models indicates that the model architectures were likely not the cause of poor performance. Rather, the deficiency in training data availability appeared to be the predominant factor. Note that this performance obtained with the proper unbiasing of both data and the evaluation procedure (see Methods) is in agreement with the results reported by the STAPLER
19
team on their proprietary experimental test dataset that was generated independently of the training data.
