In light of these results, we refined this initial analysis by stratifying the results of the unseen peptide test case based on the minimum peptide edit distance to the training set. All models demonstrated a failure to generalize effectively, as evidenced by their ROC-AUC hovering around 0.5, regardless of the edit distance, indicative of performance akin to random classifiers (Fig
2
c). Specifically, there was a discernibly weak signal for ATM-TCR at an edit distance of 1, as judged by the lower range of a 90% confidence interval (CI) located above the 0.5 threshold.
We obtained similar results from an alternative 5-fold cross-validation experiment (referred to as CV split in the following text) derived from the entire TULIP dataset (Supplementary Fig.
2
). This experiment confirmed that our results were not specific to the original way in which the TULIP dataset was split into training and testing sets. One exception was the results from the Gradient Boosting model, that showed slightly above-random lower CI at edit distance 1 in the CV split experiment, while showing <0.45 upper CI in the TULIP split experiment at the same edit distance. Considering that this model showed a random-level performance at distance bins above 1, it is likely that its generalization at edit distance 1 in the CV split is an overfitting in which this model exploited hidden correlations in the training or testing data.
As a complement to our main analysis stratified by peptide edit distance, we assessed how the number of training TCRs paired with the closest peptide in the training set affects model performance. For each test example, we identified the nearest training peptide by minimal edit distance and binned it by the number of associated TCRs from the positive examples, with bins 1–10, 11–100 and > 100. ROC-AUC was then computed across test examples in each bin for all five models. These bins were applied to both TULIP split and CV split to obtain comparable graphical representations for both experiments (Supplementary Figs.
3
and
4
), although there were very few peptides populating the 1–10 bin in the TULIP split due to its construction procedure. If we focus our attention on bins with at least 25 test peptides to avoid conclusions being driven by just a few peptides, we can see that there is a discernible positive, although weak, association between performance and training TCR count only at edit distance 1. Even at that distance, it is not entirely consistent. For example, the performance drops for ATM-TCR in the TULIP split. The nonmonotonic trends along the Y axis across neighboring edit distance bins likely reflect hidden correlations in the data and the fact that data compiled in the public databases was generated to answer biological questions specific to respective studies rather than for unbiased training of specificity prediction models.
We also observed that TULIP performance (Fig
2
c) notably deviated from that reported in the original publication
11
. To better understand the performance gap between our evaluation and the original TULIP publication, we performed a series of diagnostic tests using the pretrained TULIP checkpoint and their original test set, with both model weights and data downloaded from the original GitHub repository (
https://github.com/barthelemymp/TULIP-TCR/blob/main/data/UnseenPeptides.zip
). The results are shown in Supplementary Fig.
6
.
The first source of the discrepancy in the reported performance lies in the differences between evaluation criteria. The original TULIP publication evaluated ROC-AUC per peptide and subsequently averaged ROC-AUC across peptides. Their approach circumvented the need to estimate the marginal probability of observing a given epitope peptide inherent in their Bayesian formalism. In contrast, our study addresses a more challenging and practically relevant task: predicting binding of a given
