Discussion
Our benchmarking of published and baseline ML models confirms the importance of taking into account similarities between training and testing datasets at the TCR and epitope level, as well as considering other correlations which can confound performance evaluation. All evaluated models exhibited random or near-random performance on unseen epitopes, with large variance in AUC metrics across folds. Our results are in agreement with a recently published open source competition assessing TCR specificity prediction on unseen epitopes
20
. The cross-validation studies performed here demonstrate the ability of some architectures to generalize to epitopes highly similar to those seen during training, but not those that are dissimilar; ATM-TCR shows better-than-random performance on epitopes up to edit distance 1, and possibly 2, away from training set examples.
Our datasets constructed from public databases show much higher
