diversity than epitope diversity. When we consider previously published better-than-random results on TCR-based splits (confirmed in our benchmarking and not reported here), we conclude that the limited number of unique peptide epitopes available for training constitutes the primary bottleneck for generalization of the sequence-based TCR-pMHC specificity prediction models.
A useful TCR-pMHC specificity prediction model for personalized TCR-based immunotherapy design must be able to generalize to any possible neoantigen presented on a given patient’s MHC-I. Here, we use a representative set of 34 MHC-I alleles and an MHC-I binding predictor (NetMHCPan 4.1) to identify upper and lower estimates for the size of SNV-derived non-self ligandomes. Our lower, experimentally supported estimates suggest that 1,174,573, 172,000, and 135,064 unique epitopes are required to train a specificity model that can generalize to unseen epitopes at an edit distance of 1, 2, and 3, respectively. However, we observe strong correlations between expanded experimental ligandome sizes and the amount of public experimental data for a given allele. This suggests that our estimates for less studied alleles with few experimental data points may be large underestimations. Indeed, the theoretical 9-mer space is greater than 5*10
11
, making it unlikely any MHC-I allele will ever be exhaustively experimentally screened. Hence, we thoroughly investigated our union ligandomes for their reliability as upper bound estimates.
Interestingly, we see orders of magnitude differences in union ligandome sizes for different MHC-I alleles. The estimates for union ligandome dominating set sizes at various edit distance thresholds show no significant correlation with NetMHCPan training data or publicly available training data. Therefore, the correlation between NetMHCPan training data volume and experimental ligandome size is likely driven by separate correlations between both of these variables and the volume of public peptide-MHC data available for a given allele. Indeed, union ligandome size differences may reflect true variation in the number of peptides each MHC-I can bind. These findings are consistent with other work noting the differences in magnitude between MHC-I ligandomes
21
. Hence, our produced union ligandomes are likely reasonable upper bound estimations, suggesting that 110,392,479, 21,277,040, and 3,035,244 unique epitopes are required to train a sequence-based specificity model that can generalize to unseen epitopes at an edit distance of 1, 2, and 3, respectively. To our knowledge, this is the first exhaustive estimation of a pan-allelic neoantigen ligandome. These estimations required significant engineering and computing infrastructure; we share the results with the scientific community hoping that they will help focus further efforts in both data generation and modeling.
We note the following limitations in the work presented here. Our compilation of the available public TCR-pMHC binding data contains 1,407 unique epitopes, representing just 1.04% and 0.05% of the lower and upper estimates for data required to train a model that can generalize at edit distance 3, respectively. These estimates do not consider the possibility of ”emergent” model behavior, in which a model with the generalization distance of 1 would start generalizing at progressively longer edit distances as more and more data is added to the training set. The presence of such emergent behavior could be tested from the learning curves
22
by evaluating the generalization edit distance as a function of dataset size upon iterative subsampling, approximating with analytical form such as a power-law function and then extrapolating to future larger training sizes. As we have shown, the performance on the full set of currently available data is close to a random baseline with wide confidence intervals. That means we can only sample the very beginning of the learning curve, and extrapolation beyond the current data size will have extreme uncertainty making such an estimation non-informative. A theoretical alternative to resampling of real training data could be the generation of synthetic ground truth data. For example, TULIP has the capability to generate novel sequences of TCR binders given a target epitope. We could use TULIP or another generative model to emit synthetic binders for a subset of our simulated non-self ligandome, and then use this dataset as ground truth data to build a learning curve for ATM-TCR and other specificity prediction models as discussed above. However, we would only evaluate the capacity of a model such as ATM-TCR to learn the distribution produced by another model, the generator. It is reasonable to assume that this distribution will not capture the full biological and experimental complexity of real TCR-pMHC interactions. For example, if TULIP was able to recapitulate the diversity matching a real TCR-pMHC distribution, it would have shown generalization in specificity prediction on real test data, which in our benchmarking was not observed. As such, any scaling trends derived from synthetic data will not serve as proof of behavior on future real data, which is the object of our present study.
Furthermore, due to the size of union ligandomes, and the computational complexity of solving the minimum dominating set problem, we employed several heuristic methods in producing these estimates. The SACHICA algorithm used for edit distance clustering has been validated as highly accurate for detecting similar short strings with fixed alphabets, and is unlikely to introduce errors which affect the conclusions of this work
23
. However, the fast greedy algorithm employed for dominating set solving may return up to 2
n
nodes for a minimum dominating set of size
n
24
. Additionally, NetMHCPan 4.1 is employed here as predictive model for MHC-I binding, with a median positive predictive value reported in the literature of 0.83
8
. Hence, in a worst case scenario these union ligandomes are an overestimation of their true values by more than a factor of two. Yet, the lack of experimental data, bias in experimentally supported estimates, and computational intractablity of brute force approaches to clustering and dominating set solving means more accurate estimations are currently out of reach.
Additionally, the performance of evaluated models on epitope based splits shows large variation. This may be due to the large imbalances in the number of peptides available when stratifying by peptide similarity, differences in experimental methods used to obtain these TCR-pMHC binding pairs, and hidden correlations due to non-random selection of targets in the experiments which generated the available data. Indeed, similarly to the MHC-I binding data, public TCR-pMHC binding data is dominated by common alleles such as A*02:01 and peptides of clinical interest such as melanoma and cancer-testis antigens. Hence, while we can confidently show lack of generalization to unseen peptides, it is difficult to reliably conclude at what edit distance and associated TCR counts these models could generalize because of the lack of clearly observed trends.
This work evaluated only supervised and generative ML models which are trained by learning patterns of amino acid sequences directly from the available data of TCR-pMHC binding examples, an approach advanced across numerous publications over the last few years with some representative methods benchmarked in our work
11
,
12
,
14
. Although the alternative approaches were outside of the scope of our study, we expect that the path to building a generalizable TCR specificity prediction model against unseen epitopes will lie in building a foundational model trained to predict general protein interactions on a much larger, already available corpus of data and possibly fine-tuning it on TCR-pMHC interaction data. In particular, models which aim to predict structures and/or physical ensembles of TCR-pMHC complexes and use estimated structural quality metrics or thermodynamic averages to score the binding strength look very promising
25
, although their current performance in predicting TCR specificity is likely limited by the insufficient accuracy of structure prediction algorithms on immune receptor complexes.
Overall, it is clear from this work that despite the application of increasingly complex architectures to the TCR-pMHC specificity problem, insufficient epitope data prevents the production of a useful sequence-based model via both supervised and unsupervised learning with acceptable performance on unseen epitopes. Existing datasets cover less than 1% of the lowest estimate produced here for required data, without considering the clustering of this current data around disease-linked targets. While there will be a large cost associated with the production of this essential wet-lab data, it is becoming comparable to the computational cost spent on training complex machine learning architectures on this problem. Overall, we suggest that the main efforts in this space should be directed toward experimental generation of sufficiently large and unbiased training data to improve coverage of the TCR-pMHC binding space.
